# Trivy findings this repository has decided against, with the decision.
#
# Only rules whose answer is the same for every workload here belong in this
# file. A finding that could be right somewhere in the chart is dismissed on
# the alert instead, so it still stops a pull request when it means something.
#
# The reason this file exists rather than a growing pile of dismissals: a
# dismissal is per alert instance, and editing a template raises a fresh
# instance of everything that was already dismissed on it. Touching one
# CronJob re-raised six findings that had been triaged a week earlier, so the
# toil scales with how often the chart is edited and trains whoever is doing it
# to dismiss without reading.

# Runs with UID <= 10000
#
# Every workload here sets runAsUser 1000 with runAsNonRoot true,
# seccompProfile RuntimeDefault, allowPrivilegeEscalation false and
# capabilities drop ALL. Trivy wants a uid above 10000 so a container uid
# cannot collide with a host user account.
#
# These are single-tenant nodes with no such accounts, and 1000 is chosen to
# match the ownership of the world and backup PersistentVolumes. Raising it
# would mean rewriting ownership on volumes holding a live Minecraft world for
# no change in what the container can reach.
KSV-0020

# Runs with GID <= 10000
#
# Same decision as KSV-0020. fsGroup 2000 is what makes the world and backup
# claims readable by the non-root uid above; the number is a consequence of the
# volumes, not a default nobody looked at.
KSV-0021

# Deliberately NOT ignored, and dismissed per instance instead:
#
#   KSV-0125  restrict images to trusted registries. Everything here is
#             ghcr.io/jdwillmsen, which Trivy's default list does not know --
#             but a template pulling from a registry nobody controls is exactly
#             what this rule should stop, so it stays able to.
#
#   KSV-0049  manage configmaps. The backup job's grant is scoped by
#             resourceNames to the single metrics ConfigMap it writes, and only
#             `create` is unscoped because Kubernetes RBAC cannot scope it. An
#             unscoped grant somewhere else would be a real finding.
#
#   KSV-0053  exec into pods. Inherent: Bedrock has no RCON, so pods/exec is
#             the only route to the server console.
#
#   KSV-0014  read-only root filesystem. Fixed rather than ignored, in #113.
